    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Can you go into a bit more detail on when we'd choose one over the other? Specifically, if we're expecting unpredictable spikes in traffic, which one is generally better for stability?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under unpredictable traffic spikes, horizontal scaling is usually the better choice for maintaining stability. With vertical scaling, if you only have one server, it may become a single point of failure or become overwhelmed during those spikes. On the other hand, horizontal scaling spreads the load across multiple servers, providing better resilience and scaling capabilities. This approach also allows for more seamless scaling to accommodate sudden increases in traffic without compromising the performance of your service. However, it's also worth noting that horizontal scaling might introduce complexity in terms of load balancing and synchronization across servers.",
      "source": "target",
