    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so with horizontal scaling, what are some of the common things that can go wrong or cause issues when you're trying to manage all those different servers at once? like what are the typical bottlenecks or risks?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Common issues with horizontal scaling include the complexity of managing multiple servers, ensuring consistent configuration across them, and effectively distributing traffic among them. A typical bottleneck can occur with the load balancing system, which needs to be properly configured to evenly distribute traffic and prevent any single server from being overloaded. Other risks include network latency between distributed servers and potential inconsistencies in data if not managed correctly—for example, using sticky sessions without proper synchronization. Additionally, monitoring and diagnosing issues can be more challenging with horizontal scaling, as you'd have to track multiple points in your infrastructure to isolate problems. It's important to implement robust automation and monitoring tools to mitigate these risks and ensure smooth operations.",
      "source": "target",
